    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Despite the widespread availability of the COVID-19 vaccine in the United States, 30% of\r\nhealthcare personnel (HCP) were still not vaccinated by the end of December, 2021. The purpose\r\nof my thesis was to identify characteristics of hospital-based healthcare personnel that are\r\nassociated with COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and with changing one’s mind about getting\r\nvaccinated; knowledge of the types of healthcare personnel who are vaccine hesitant will help\r\nour public health system better address gaps in HCP vaccine coverage. I analyzed surveys from\r\nover 15,000 hospital-based HCP from Stanford Healthcare in December 2020 to understand\r\nemployee intentions with respect to COVID-19 vaccination. I found that female healthcare\r\nworkers, Black / African American healthcare workers, and younger healthcare workers were\r\nmore likely to delay or refuse the vaccine. HCP from counties of high social vulnerability and\r\nHCP that interface less with patients also showed higher rates of vaccine hesitancy and higher\r\nrates of changing their minds about getting vaccinated. Furthermore, vaccine newness and fear of\r\nside effects were the most cited reasons for vaccine hesitancy. Healthcare workers in sub-groups\r\nwith high rates of vaccine hesitancy should be specially considered in the design of future\r\nvaccination campaigns for hospital workers.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
